      "id": "w7krds1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Force"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Force is a vector quantity that can alter an object's state of rest, direction, or speed.",
        "Newton's First Law introduces inertia, Second Law gives the formula F = ma, and Third Law establishes action-reaction pairs.",
        "Work is calculated as W = F × d × cos(θ), where θ is the angle between force and displacement vectors.",
        "Work can be positive (θ < 90°), negative (θ > 90°), or zero (θ = 90° or no displacement).",
        "Power represents the rate of doing work (P = W / t) and is measured in Watts."
      ],
      "id": "y8q053j"
    },
